# How to run the CM1_LETKF system
# updated 05/2025
#

0.  Required software:

    see the letkf_env.yml file 

1.  Compilation.

a)  compile the cm1r21v1 code. If you want to use older versions of CM1, you will need to make a number of edits in ens.py and run_fcst.py (possibly others). Older versions of CM1 have differently named restart files (cm1_rst_XXXXXX vs cm1rst_XXXX) and the restart files also don't have a time dimension, so they are 3D rather than 4D. Note that in the provided version of CM1, Statspack.F and Misclibs.F have been altered to output pressure tendency proxies, domean mean qr, and mean 5-km AGL vertical velocity. If stats are output at every timestep (in the cm1out_stats.nc file), you can calculate dp/ds and weak temperature gradient w residuals for model balance metrics.

b)  compile the code in fsrc:  *** this needs work because it has been manually edited to point to my conda environment ***

    type fcompile.py, make sure at the end you get:

     ==========================================================================================


     Successfully compiled file: fpython2.f90

     Object file is: fpython2.so

        --> fpython2 was successfully imported into python, you should be good to go...

     
     ==========================================================================================

   If you get a series of link errors complaining about x86_64 incompatibilities, cp "Plotting/gnu.py" into
   "~user/anaconda/lib/python2.7/site-packages/numpy/distutils/fcompiler/." as this should fix it. Try the compile
   again.


2.  The main python script to edit is namelist.py. This is a typical namelist but in python format to take advantage of datetime object syntax. When that has been edited, cd into the run directory and type "./RUN.sh >> letkf.out". This runs the experiment_driver.py script in the src directory, which manages the other scripts and reads in the applicable variables from the namelist. 

To edit the assimilated data and/or the base state sounding(s), make sure the obs are placed in the observations/ directory (in run/) in the proper csv format, and fill the soundings/ directory (in run/) with appropriately named soundings (RunXXSounding.txt, where XX corresponds to the 0-padded member number).


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Overview of how the program works:

The python script create_run_letkf.py reads in the namelist, puts the important values into a json file which is saved in the base directory (it is used to drive the rest of the experiment), and sets up the entire data structure. Structure is:

base directory/
        member000/ - contains the mean ensemble data
        member00n/ - contains all of the restart cm1 files for that member. when DA starts, the rst files are copied to a "prior" file and the rst is edited to become the posterior
        soundings/ - contains all of the soundings used to initialize the different members
        plots/     - contains all the plots (if make_plots is set to True in namelist.py), otherwise it is empty
        Inflation....nc - all the inflation netcdfs (for each DA timestep) if adaptive inflation is used
        Prior ......nc - all the prior netcdfs (for each DA timestep) that contains the radar data and the Hxf (model data at obs locations, using the forward operator calculated in ComputeHx.py which calls the calcHx function in ens.py )
        Copies of the namelist, onefile.F, cm1.exe, and landuse file (these are linked to all of the member folders)
        letkf.out - at the end of the experiment, the outfile is copied from the run directory to here
        letkf.exp - the json file created by creat_run_letkf.py. 

In a normal experiment, calc_stats.py will run, which will result in additional files called "Posterior .... nc" (for each DA timestep, identical to Prior...nc but using the posterior model data), AllPost_prior.nc which merges the Prior and Posterior files together (np.nan padded as each timestep will have a different number of obs), full_ens_fcst.nc which includes all of the member forecasts merged into a single file and also contains 2-5 km AGL UH (makes probabilistic plots wayyyy faster), and balance.nc (which contains pressure tendencies and weak thermal gradient calculations [from Diefenbach et al. 2024; to use this data, you will need to do a control run without any DA] which were pulled from the cm1out_stats.nc file from each member).


3.  To Run a forecast, edit the namelist_forecast.py script, and type "./RUN_FORECAST.SH >> forecast.out". This runs the forecast_driver.py script. The output is similar to the main experiment, except that the json file is called "fcst.exp" and there will be no posterior or prior files created. 


   




